  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe early afternoon water vapor loop and objective analysis fields \nshowed western Kansas in between the two branches of the polar \nwesterlies. The southern branch was active well to the south across \nSouth Texas and the northern branch active across southwestern \nCanada and adjacent Pacific Northwest region. The pressure gradient \nacross western Kansas was rather weak as a result, thus surface \nwinds remained light at 10 mph or less.\n\nThe split flow pattern will continue through much of the workweek, \nand this will yield more tranquil weather, outside of the occasional \nwind shifts in direction and speed. A cold front Wednesday will be \ndownslope-modified, so the pullback in temperature will be \nnegligible with late week afternoon highs generally in the upper 50s \nto lower 60s. Since there will be no major air mass change toward \nthe colder, the prospects of any long duration sub-freezing \ntemperatures are extremely low through the entirety of this forecast \nperiod (ending 15 November). This is not all a surprise in such a \nstrongly positive ENSO base state. There are some signals of a \npattern change in the D+8 to +10 time frame with deep trough out \nwest again, and this is probably the earliest we would begin to even \nthink about decent precipitation chances entering the forecast \nagain. Until then, though, temperatures will continue to be above \nnormal for both highs and lows with zero or near-zero\nprecipitation.",
